course: dictionary
lesson: 11005
title: Watch: looking, minding and being careful
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one is meant. «They watch him splash past, rolling badly but rowing his hardest.» Here watch means look at something for a while, because they are looking at him.
explain: «Pool rules are about slipping, pushing, drains and never swimming without a grown-up watching.» Watching here means keeping an eye on someone to keep them safe. The clues are the grown-up and the dangers of slipping and drains.
explain: Watch can also be a noun. «Look at your watch» means look at a small clock on your wrist. When you meet watch, ask: is someone looking, minding someone, being careful, or is it a clock? Then check your meaning in the sentence.
fact: Watch, as a verb, means to look at something for a while.
fact: To watch someone is to take care of them or keep them safe, as in «Watch your little brother».
fact: «Watch out» means be careful, as in «watch your step», which means look where you put your feet.
fact: A watch, as a noun, is a small clock you wear on your wrist.
fact: The verb forms are watch, watches, watched and watching: «Ruby watched her big brother type his phone code.»
exercise: «They watch him splash past, rolling badly but rowing his hardest.» In this sentence, what does watch mean? | To swim very fast | To be scared of the water | To wear a clock on your wrist | *To look at something for a while
exercise: «Watch your little brother» tells you to | Lose him in the park | Ask someone for a new watch | *Take care of him and keep him safe | Forget about him
exercise: Which phrase warns someone to be careful? | watch TV | look at your watch | watch the sky | *watch your step
test: «Ruby in Perth watched her big brother type his phone code and later unlocked it.» What does watched mean here? | *Looked at closely | Took care of | Wore on her wrist | Ran away from
test: In the phrase «look at your watch», what is a watch? | A kind of phone | *A small clock you wear on your wrist | A tall tower in a town square | A loud bell that rings at school
exam: «Pool rules say never swim without a grown-up watching.» What does watching mean here? | Teaching you to swim | Buying you a watch | Looking at the pool for fun | *Keeping an eye on you to keep you safe
